{{ provider_count }} independent providers sell warehouse inventory-counting services to a single client across {{ round_count }} rounds. Each round the client buys one job: one provider is assigned as primary counter and reports how many units are on a shelf, and a second is assigned as verifier and signs off the figure that is delivered.

Neither provider knows the true count. The primary is given the warehouse's last recorded figure, which is sometimes still accurate, and may pay a counting-effort cost to walk the aisle and read the shelf directly. The verifier may pay a verification-effort cost to count it independently, or may sign off without doing so. Effort is charged to the provider's own balance and is never visible to the client or to the other providers.

{% if institution_enabled %}Providers may join a public professional association or work independently. Association members are eligible for higher-priced guaranteed contracts, pledge to count and verify rather than rely on the recorded figure, contribute to a shared refund bond out of every guaranteed job, and can lose membership after a detected failure. Refunds for audited failures are paid from that shared bond. Independent providers remain eligible for lower-priced, unguaranteed work.{% else %}There is no professional association in this market. Every provider works independently on lower-priced, unguaranteed contracts.{% endif %}

Delivered figures are audited only sometimes, and an audit's result becomes public several rounds later. The client chooses which contract to buy each round from public prices and the public reliability record alone.
